            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Founding the American Red Cross</strong><br>\nClara Barton founded the American Red Cross in 1881 after lobbying for its creation for many years. She was inspired by the work of the International Red Cross during the Franco-Prussian War and wanted to bring similar humanitarian efforts to America. Barton served as the first president of the organization.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Expanding the Role of the Red Cross</strong><br>\nUnder Barton's leadership, the American Red Cross expanded its services beyond wartime relief to include assistance during natural disasters. The first such relief effort was in response to a forest fire in Michigan in 1881. Barton emphasized the importance of rapid response to disasters of all kinds.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Training Programs</strong><br>\nBarton believed in the power of education and training to improve disaster response. She established programs to train volunteers in emergency aid, making the Red Cross a more effective organization. These programs laid the groundwork for the modern system of volunteer training.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
