          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>International Influence</strong><br>\nClara Barton represented the American Red Cross at international conferences and helped to expand the organization's role in global relief efforts. She became a leading figure in the international humanitarian community and used her influence to advocate for peace and aid for the suffering.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Late Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nClara Barton continued to work with the Red Cross until 1904, when she resigned due to disagreements within the organization. She spent her remaining years giving speeches and writing about her experiences. Barton died on April 12, 1912, but left behind a powerful legacy of service and compassion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Barton's Enduring Impact</strong><br>\nThe impact of Clara Barton's work is still felt today. The American Red Cross has grown into a major humanitarian organization, thanks in large part to Barton's early efforts. Her life's work has inspired countless volunteers to serve and help those in need, both in America and around the world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
